WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:06.223
- I've been diving since 1963.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.300
I still get that thrill.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.403
When I jump off the boat in the water.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:13.800
I look at the shipwreck.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.930
I think about the history it represents.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.620
It's a thrill.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.400
I only champion the cause
of protecting shipwrecks

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.740
to let people appreciate that a shipwreck

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.500
is really in the environment;

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.550
it's part of the environment that need

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.500
to be protected and they
ought to be accessible

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.050
to the public to appreciate ‘em.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.890
My name is Professor Charles Beeker.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.630
I'm an underwater archeologist

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.570
and a specialist for
marine-protected areas.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:45.800
Hold on to that sucker.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.310
I got a chance to go scuba diving

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.210
and immediately fell in love with it.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.850
Bought my first equipment in 1966.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.100
By the ‘80s, I got
involved in academics,

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.540
and I realized that these
shipwrecks are really finite.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:57.373
Going in.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.120
They're a porthole to our past.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.040
This is a one-time event,
and it's a trapped fragment

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.020
of life for the people on board a ship

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.680
when the shipwreck goes down

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.643
that tells us about that time period.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.230
We're at the Underwater Cultural

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.680
Patrimony Conservation Laboratory.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.220
This is the national
laboratory that is involved

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.393
with the conservation of the
artifacts that are brought up.

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:30.130
This is just a fantastic object
from the days of Columbus.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.370
This is a sundial and you can see

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.860
the markings on the
dial, the Roman numerals.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.410
What I saw was numerous
shipwrecks that were

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.150
heavily impacted by either hobby divers

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.400
or professional salvage operations.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.710
You go to the actual sites

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.610
and what you find there
is relatively little left.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.880
If everybody went out
and picked up one object,

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.613
there wouldn't be anything left
for us to see in the future.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.110
These shipwrecks really
deserve being protected.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.430
I've really spent
my time trying to look

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.440
at a way and make sure that
another 30 years from now

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.240
others will have a chance to
see these finite resources.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.730
I've been coming here to
the Dominican Republic

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:12.770
to work with the government

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.930
to establish our first living museum site.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.660
We're showing that alternative
to taking these sites

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.950
and turning them into a resource

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.818
that's recognized and will be protected,

00:02:22.818 --> 00:02:24.600
rather than a resource that's going

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.480
to be harvested and disappear.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.313
And are you ready?

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:28.630
These sites are going to last.

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:29.580
They will be around.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.550
They're not going away.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.240
I'll be gone, they'll still be here,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.790
and that's really a
contribution I'm very proud of.

